Z||000008||mrfiero||02-17-2001||08:19 PM||||Not necessarily.  The V-6 engine was optional on the SE models ('85-'87).  I owned a fully loaded '85 SE (even had T-Tops), but it had a 4-cyl.  <P>You'll have to look at it in person to verify what engine it has, or look for the RPO code L44....this signifies a V-6 engine. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||02-17-2001||08:29 PM||||I looked at one last year, and I could tell it was a V6 cause one of the vents was missing and I could reach right into the engine compartment..... that was one of the many problems this car had.. and the guy wanted $3500 for it.<P>Until then, I wasn't aware that 85 SEs could be ordered with a V6 either.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Kelvin Vivian||02-18-2001||12:54 AM||||Fformula88 posted "We've got an 85 SE 2m6! Unlike later SE's, this one still has the same front and rear fascias as the 84-86 4 cyl coupes. Its a real sleeper!!"<P>The opposite of a sleeper would be an 86 SE or an 87 SE with a 4-cyl engine.  They have the dual exhausts of the V6, and the same body as the 85 GT...<P>Kelvin<BR>87 SE 4-cyl<BR>88 GT T-top<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/SouthBeach/Cove/2449/finalfront.jpg">  1987 SE - 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||02-18-2001||01:08 AM||||What's the general price range for an 86-87 SE with a 4cyl auto? also, how common are they? I've NEVER seen one for sale.. and didn't even know they existed until quite recently..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000012||Kelvin Vivian||02-19-2001||02:59 AM||||The prices of 86 and 87 SE 4-cyls are generally lower than their V6 counter parts, even though there are fewer 86 & 87 SEs with the 4-cyl engine...<P>For my little Fiero collection, I specifically looked for an 87 SE 4-cyl... In 87, Fieros were no longer designated with 2M4 or 2M6 insignia, 87 and 88 Fieros just say "Fiero" on the rear. I think factory dual exhausts for a 4-cyl Fiero is kind of neat. When I downshift, I hear the burble that a V6 would make. It doesn't have the punch of the V6, but I like it the same.<P>Kelvin <BR>87 SE 4-cyl<BR>88 GT T-top||||reg||1
